<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Geoffrey Chaucer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1343–<a href="/years/1400">1400</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Geoffrey Chaucer was an English poet and royal official, often called the father of English literature. Working as a courtier, diplomat and customs controller in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, he wrote Troilus and Criseyde and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-canterbury-tales">The Canterbury Tales</a> in the London dialect of Middle English, helping to make English a literary language alongside French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. He was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geoffrey-chaucer.jpg" alt="Geoffrey Chaucer" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaucer_manuscrit_portrait_(d%C3%A9tail).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
